<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Horio Yoshiharu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1542">1542</a>–<a href="/years/1611">1611</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Horio Yoshiharu was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> who served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> and then sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> his family received Izumo and Oki, and he directed the building of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsue">Matsue</a> castle on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-shinji">Lake Shinji</a>, completed in 1611, the year he died. The Horio line died out in 1633.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/horio-yoshiharu.jpg" alt="Horio Yoshiharu" width="320" height="703" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown (Inscription by Shunryō Gensai) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Horio_Yoshiharu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Horio Yoshiharu, also known as Horio Mosuke, lived through the Azuchi–Momoyama and early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo periods</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>, then called Hashiba Hideyoshi, took him into service after seeing him wrestle a wild boar. Yoshiharu was serving Hideyoshi when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gifu-castle">Inabayama Castle</a> in 1567. In 1582, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tenmokuzan">Battle of Tenmokuzan</a>, he executed Oyamada Nobushige, and when Hideyoshi besieged Bitchū Takamatsu Castle he conducted the inquest into the death of Shimizu Muneharu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Nobunaga's death Yoshiharu rose as one of Hideyoshi's retainers. He killed Saito Toshikazu at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yamazaki">Battle of Yamazaki</a> and fought at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-shizugatake">Battle of Shizugatake</a> in 1583, in the Toyama campaign against Sassa Narimasa in 1585 and in the Kyūshū Campaign of 1587. For his part in the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a>, Hideyoshi gave him 120,000 koku at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamamatsu">Hamamatsu</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totomi-province">Tōtōmi Province</a> in 1590, and in his last years appointed him, with Nakamura Kazuuji and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikoma-chikamasa">Ikoma Chikamasa</a>, one of the three chūrō, the arbiters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> administration.</p><p>After Hideyoshi died in 1598, Yoshiharu went over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>. In 1599 he handed the headship of his family to his son, Horio Tadauji, and retired to a domain of 50,000 koku at Fuchu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/echizen-province">Echizen Province</a>. In 1600 he killed Kaganoi Shigemochi at Chiryu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikawa-province">Mikawa Province</a> but was wounded, and so missed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a>. Ieyasu nevertheless gave the family 240,000 koku at Toda in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izumo-province">Izumo Province</a>, since Tadauji had fought at the battle on his father's behalf.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tadauji died young in 1604, and his son Horio Tadaharu, then nine years old, succeeded him. Yoshiharu governed as his guardian until his own death on 26 July 1611. Calm and well liked, he was called Hotoke no Mosuke, Mosuke the saint. He was the first leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsue">Matsue</a> clan. The family temple of the Horio clan is the Shunkō-in, at Hanazono in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Horio Yoshiharu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Sekigahara, Tokugawa Ieyasu gave Izumo and Oki to <b>Horio Yoshiharu</b> in 1600.</blockquote><a href="/state/matsue-domain">Matsue Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Oguchi, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.33,136.91&amp;year=1586" aria-label="Oguchi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8014 886 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Horio Yoshiharu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8464 1156h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8464 1156h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.33,136.91&amp;year=1586">What was here in 1586? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
